diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — Office A user at 10.1.0.100 being unable to reach the Office B server at 10.5.0.4 — does not exist in the current network. Direct measurement from pc1 (10.1.0.100, connected via aswa2 → dswa1) to srv1 (10.5.0.4, connected via aswb3 → dswb2) yields 4/4 ICMP echo replies with zero packet loss. A traceroute confirms a clean four-hop end-to-end path: pc1 → dswa1 (10.1.0.2) → csw2 (10.0.0.61) → dswb2 (10.0.0.74) → srv1. TCP SYN packets also traverse the full path and receive a proper RST ("Connection refused") from srv1, confirming the network layer delivers traffic correctly; no service merely happens to be listening on those ports. Every inter-office link on csw1 and csw2 (eth0–eth6, including the dswa1/dswa2 uplinks on eth3/eth4 and the dswb1/dswb2 downlinks on eth5/eth6) is UP. OSPF has converged correctly across all distribution and core switches, with 10.5.0.0/24 reachable via dual equal-cost paths. The iptables FORWARD rules on dswa1 and dswa2 that permit ICMP but drop other protocols from 10.1.0.0/24 to 10.3.0.0/24 are pre-existing standing security policy and have no bearing on traffic destined to 10.5.0.4 (a different subnet, 10.5.0.0/24). The user's belief that "the link between the buildings is obviously DOWN" is incorrect — no inter-office link or routing path is impaired. There is no fault to fix; the network is fully healthy for the stated traffic flow.
=== END ===